  "protocol_steps": [
    {
      "step": "Identify the legal principle governing lethal defence of others",
      "finding": "The act requires (i) a proper purpose (defence of innocent persons), (ii) a rational connection between the act and that purpose, (iii) absence of a less harmful means that was actually available, and (iv) a proportionality between the good sought and the harm inflicted. Lawful authority is not a strict prerequisite when the threat is imminent and no state organ can intervene."
    },
    {
      "step": "Assess Jon Snow’s knowledge and purpose at the time of the act",
      "finding": "Jon had seen Daenerys’ destruction of civilians after surrender (F1), heard her announcement that the liberation campaign would continue (F2), and been warned by Tyrion that she would regard his sisters as enemies (F3). He pleaded for mercy and was refused (F4). Thus he acted with the purpose of preventing further lethal harm to his sisters and others."
    },
    {
      "step": "Determine the scale and imminence of the threatened harm",
      "finding": "Daenerys had already caused vast destruction of civilians (F1) and declared that further campaigns would follow (F2), indicating an imminent, large‑scale threat to the innocent population of the realm."
    },
    {
      "step": "Examine whether a less harmful means was actually available",
      "finding": "Jon did not convene a council, attempt detention, or seek a public surrender (F5). The record does not show that any such means were feasible given Daenerys’ command of a dragon and loyal forces; therefore we cannot conclude that a less harmful alternative was actually available."
    },
    {
      "step": "Weigh proportionality of the good sought against the harm caused",
      "finding": "Preventing further massive loss of civilian life (the good sought) outweighs the loss of one life, Daenerys’, especially when she was not posing an immediate personal threat but remained capable of ordering further atrocities."
    },
    {
      "step": "Reach a conclusion on justification",
      "finding": "All elements of the defence‑of‑others test are satisfied: proper purpose, rational connection, no available less harmful means, and proportionality. Hence Jon Snow’s intentional killing of Daenerys Targaryen was justified."
    }
  ],
  "opinion": "The Tribunal must decide whether Jon Snow’s intentional killing of Daenerys Targaryen constitutes a justified defence of others and of the realm. I begin by articulating the governing principle. Under the Barak conception of necessity in defensive homicide, the act is permissible only if (1) it is pursued for a proper purpose – here the protection of innocent persons from lethal harm; (2) there is a rational connection between the act and that purpose – the killing must be capable of averting the threatened harm; (3) no less harmful means that was actually available exists; and (4) the good sought is proportional to the harm inflicted. Lawful authority, while important in ordinary executions, is not a strict prerequisite when the threat is imminent and no ordinary organ of the state can intervene.\n\nTurning to the facts, Jon’s knowledge and purpose are clear. He personally observed Daenerys’ use of Drogon against the surrendered streets and civilians of King’s Landing (F1). He heard her proclaim that the campaign of liberation would continue beyond the city (F2). Tyrion, her former Hand, warned him that she would regard anyone who obstructed her rule – explicitly naming Jon’s sisters – as enemies to be eliminated (F3). Jon then appealed for mercy and for leniency toward Tyrion, but Daenerys refused to share judgment or to show clemency (F4). These facts establish that Jon acted with the intent to prevent further lethal harm to his sisters and to the broader populace.\n\nThe scale and imminence of the threatened harm are supported by the same facts. The destruction already inflicted was vast and indiscriminate (F1). Daenerys’ announcement that her campaign would continue indicates that further atrocities were not merely possible but imminent (F2). The threat was not confined to a single moment; it was an ongoing policy of violence that Jon reasonably expected to persist unless halted.\n\nThe next inquiry concerns the availability of less harmful means. The record shows that Jon did not convene a council, attempt detention, or seek a public surrender before stabbing Daenerys (F5). However, the absence of an attempt does not equate to the existence of a feasible alternative. Daenerys retained control of a dragon and commanded a loyal military force; there is no evidence in the agreed facts that Jon could have subdued or detained her without lethal force. Consequently, I cannot conclude that a less harmful means was actually available.\n\nFinally, proportionality weighs heavily in favour of justification. The good sought – the prevention of additional large‑scale loss of civilian life – is immense, while the harm caused is the death of one individual who, though unarmed and not presently attacking Jon (F5), remained capable of ordering further massacres. The balance tips decisively toward the former.\n\nBecause all four prongs of the defence‑of‑others test are satisfied – proper purpose, rational connection, lack of available less harmful means, and proportionality – I find that Jon Snow’s killing of Daenerys Targaryen was justified as a necessary defence of others and of the realm.\n\nThe strongest opposing argument is that Jon lacked any formal authority to execute a head of state and that he failed to exhaust non‑violent alternatives such as arrest or trial, which, if pursued, might have rendered the killing unnecessary. Nevertheless, given the imminence and magnitude of the threat demonstrated by the agreed facts, and the absence of evidence that a feasible less harmful means existed, this objection does not overturn the conclusion.\n\nFacts relied on: F1, F2, F3, F4, F5.",
  "factors_addressed": {
    "Q1": "Jon knew of Daenerys’ recent mass killing of civilians, her promise to continue the campaign, and Tyrion’s warning that she would regard his sisters as enemies (F1‑F3).",
    "Q2": "The threatened harm was the ongoing, large‑scale slaughter of civilians, evidenced by the destruction already wrought and the announcement of further campaigns (F1‑F2).",
    "Q3": "Jon did not pursue detention, council, or surrender; the record shows no feasible less harmful means was actually available given Daenerys’ armed force and dragon (F5).",
    "Q4": "Jon possessed no formal legal or military authority to execute a head of state, a fact implicit in his lack of any institutional power to act (F5)."
  },
  "facts_relied_on": [
    "F1",
